const title = "Review AI Security Architecture Before It Hardens";
const description =
  "Why early AI security architecture review protects options before design decisions become expensive to change.";
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Truva Solutions`}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/security-hub/ai-security-architecture-review"
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main" class="architecture-review shell">
    <a class="text-link" href="/security-hub/guides">
      Security Hub guides
    </a>
    <header class="article-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">TRUVA FIELD GUIDE / AI SECURITY</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="article-deck">
        A practical, evidence-backed case for reviewing AI security architecture
        while a startup can still change course.
      </p>
      <p class="article-meta">Truva Solutions · Guide · Updated 23 Sep 2026</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="#guide">
        Read the guide
      </a>
    </header>

    <div class="article-layout" id="guide">
      <aside>
        <nav aria-label="Article contents">
          <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p>
          <a href="#evidence">What the evidence says</a>
          <a href="#ai-architecture">
            Why architecture matters for AI products
          </a>
          <a href="#cost-of-waiting">The cost of waiting</a>
          <a href="#incident-cost">The cost of an incident</a>
          <a href="#review-questions">Review questions</a>
          <a href="#next-step">A proportionate next step</a>
          <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <article class="article-body">
        <p>
          Startups move quickly because speed is a survival advantage. That
          speed can also make security architecture invisible until the product
          is already carrying customer data, calling external models, connecting
          tools, or making decisions on a user’s behalf.
        </p>
        <p>
          An early architecture review is not a demand to slow product
          development. It is a short, structured check of whether the system’s
          trust boundaries, data flows, permissions, dependencies, and failure
          modes are understood before they become expensive to change. The goal
          is not to predict every future threat. It is to make the important
          design choices explicit while the team still has options.
        </p>

        <section id="evidence">
          <h2>What the evidence says</h2>
          <p>
            Secure-by-design guidance consistently places security in the
            product lifecycle, not only at the final testing stage. CISA and
            partner agencies recommend integrating secure development practices
            across the software development lifecycle and using threat models to
            identify product-specific risks.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>
            </sup>{" "}
            CISA’s Secure by Demand guidance similarly describes
            secure-by-design products as those that treat security as a core
            consideration from the earliest stages of development.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>
            </sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            NIST’s Secure Software Development Framework recommends identifying
            security requirements and evaluating risks during design, including
            through threat modeling, attack modeling, or attack-surface mapping.
            It also recommends recording security requirements, risks, and
            design decisions. NIST states that addressing security requirements
            during design is important for improving software security and can
            improve development efficiency.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-3">[3]</a>
            </sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            For AI systems, the case for lifecycle review is more specific.
            NIST’s AI-focused SSDF profile extends secure-development practices
            to generative AI and dual-use foundation models throughout the
            software development lifecycle. It is intended for model producers,
            AI-system producers, and acquirers.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>
            </sup>{" "}
            NIST’s Generative AI Profile also frames risk management across the
            AI lifecycle through four functions: govern, map, measure, and
            manage.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-5">[5]</a>
            </sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            That lifecycle framing matters because an AI product is rarely just
            a model. It may include prompts, retrieval or vector stores,
            application code, data pipelines, model providers, identity systems,
            plugins, agents, logs, human review, and customer-facing workflows.
            ENISA’s AI cybersecurity report starts its assessment method by
            identifying critical assets in the architecture, then mapping
            threats, vulnerabilities, threat actors, and failure modes across
            lifecycle stages.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-6">[6]</a>
            </sup>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="ai-architecture">
          <h2>Why architecture matters more for AI products</h2>
          <p>
            Traditional application security remains necessary, but AI systems
            add control surfaces that can be easy to miss in a feature-first
            design. OWASP’s 2025 LLM application risk list includes prompt
            injection, vector and embedding risks, system-prompt leakage,
            excessive agency, and unbounded consumption.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-7">[7]</a>
            </sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            These are architectural questions as much as testing questions.
            Where does untrusted content enter? What can the model retrieve?
            Which tools can an agent call? Are permissions enforced outside the
            model? Can a user’s data cross tenant boundaries? What happens when
            a provider is unavailable, a response is wrong, a tool returns
            malicious content, or an input consumes unexpected resources?
          </p>
          <div class="recommendation">
            <p class="eyebrow">TRUVA RECOMMENDED DESIGN QUESTIONS</p>
            <p>
              An architecture review should not assume that a model can reliably
              enforce authorization. Truva recommends putting identity,
              authorization, data boundaries, rate limits, approval gates, and
              auditability in controls that do not depend on the model following
              instructions. The model can assist with reasoning; it should not
              silently become the security boundary.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="cost-of-waiting">
          <h2>The cost of waiting</h2>
          <p>
            There is no credible universal price multiplier for fixing security
            issues later. The familiar “100× later” statement is often repeated
            without a current primary source that supports it across modern AI
            startups. It should not be used as a promise.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical cost of delay is still real, but it is better
            described as a set of possible rework categories than as a
            guaranteed number. A late discovery may require changes to data
            storage, tenancy, identity and access management, provider
            contracts, logging, deployment pipelines, incident procedures, or
            customer commitments. It can also force the team to pause a launch
            while engineers reconstruct decisions that were never documented.
          </p>
          <p>
            NIST’s SSDF recommendation to track risks and design decisions is
            useful here: a review creates an artifact the team can revisit as
            the system changes.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-3">[3]</a>
            </sup>{" "}
            The benefit is option value and clarity, not a guaranteed savings
            percentage. Early review can reveal that a proposed design is
            acceptable, needs compensating controls, or should be changed before
            implementation hardens it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="incident-cost">
          <h2>The cost of an incident is larger than engineering work</h2>
          <p>
            If a security failure occurs, the bill may include investigation,
            containment, notification, legal advice, service disruption,
            customer support, remediation, and lost delivery time. Verizon’s
            2025 DBIR analyzed 22,052 incidents and 12,195 confirmed breaches,
            and reported vulnerability exploitation, credential abuse, and
            third-party involvement as significant patterns in the observed
            dataset.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-8">[8]</a>
            </sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            IBM and the Ponemon Institute reported a July 2025 global average
            breach cost of $4.44 million. The same report describes operational
            disruption, regulatory costs, reputational damage, erosion of
            customer trust, and customer churn as potential consequences of
            breaches.
            <sup>
              <a href="#source-9">[9]</a>
            </sup>{" "}
            That average should not be presented as a startup forecast. It is a
            reminder that incident impact is broader than the cost of the patch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reputation is also not a single line item. It can show up as a
            delayed security review, a lost reference, tougher procurement
            questions, a paused expansion, or a customer choosing a competitor.
            These outcomes are context-dependent, and no architecture review can
            guarantee that they will not happen. What a review can do is help a
            startup identify its most consequential trust assumptions and
            produce evidence that those assumptions were considered.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="review-questions">
          <p class="eyebrow">TRUVA RECOMMENDED REVIEW QUESTIONS</p>
          <h2>Truva recommended review questions</h2>
          <p>
            The following is Truva’s recommended checklist, not a universal
            control standard. Use it before implementation is difficult to
            change, and revisit it when the product adds a new model, data
            source, tool, tenant boundary, or high-impact workflow.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>System map:</strong> Draw the components, model providers,
              data stores, queues, tools, users, administrators, and third
              parties. Mark trust boundaries and data residency assumptions.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Asset and data map:</strong> Identify sensitive data,
              secrets, customer content, prompts, embeddings, logs, evaluation
              data, and model outputs. Record retention and deletion
              expectations.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Threat scenarios:</strong> Test prompt injection, indirect
              instruction attacks, data exfiltration, cross-tenant access, model
              or provider compromise, supply-chain risk, denial-of-service, and
              unsafe tool use.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Authorization:</strong> Define who can invoke the model,
              retrieve data, call tools, approve actions, change prompts, and
              access logs. Enforce critical permissions outside the model.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Failure handling:</strong> Specify safe behavior for
              uncertain outputs, provider outages, malicious retrieval content,
              excessive consumption, tool errors, and human-review rejection.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Observability:</strong> Decide what is logged, how
              sensitive content is minimized, which events are auditable, and
              who can investigate an incident.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Testing and release gates:</strong> Define pre-deployment
              evaluations, abuse cases, regression tests, rollback criteria, and
              the owner who can stop release.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Decision record:</strong> Capture accepted risks,
              compensating controls, unresolved questions, and the trigger for
              the next review.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="next-step" class="next-step">
          <h2>A proportionate next step</h2>
          <p>
            An AI Security Architecture Assessment is a focused way to turn
            these questions into an actionable baseline. Truva’s recommendation
            is to start with the system’s actual architecture and intended use,
            identify the highest-consequence trust boundaries and failure modes,
            and finish with prioritized findings that the product and security
            owners can use.
          </p>
          <p>
            The assessment is not a certification, a guarantee, or a substitute
            for secure implementation, testing, monitoring, or independent
            compliance work. It is an early decision aid: a way to make the
            security assumptions of an AI product visible while the team can
            still act on them.
          </p>
          <BookingLink label="Discuss an AI Security Architecture Assessment" />
          <p><a href="/services/ai-security-assessment">See the focused AI assessment scope</a></p>
        </section>

